{"type":"message","id":"3c312633","parentId":"36290da0","timestamp":"2026-10-20T04:08:19.305Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`sort_a` is faster: it took about 0.169 s on 100k random ints, against about 0.427 s for `sort_b`, roughly 2.5× quicker. `RESULT.md` now contains the line `faster: sort_a`.\n\n**How I measured:** both functions sorted the same list of 100,000 random ints (seed 0, values from 0 to 10⁹). I ran each three times and kept the fastest run, and checked that both outputs match `sorted(xs)`.\n\n**Why:** `sort_a` is a merge sort, so its work grows as n log n. `sort_b` calls `bisect.insort` once per element, and each insert shifts part of the list, so its work grows as n². At 100k, Python's fast C list shifting keeps `sort_b` close, but the gap will widen on bigger inputs."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":268,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":37365,"totalTokens":37635,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":37365}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792469296039,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011CgCqBbYeaZw54tXgm1uND","duration":3266.4747970000008,"ttft":3241.7197720000004,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":37367,"nonMessageTokens":18238}}}
